messages.text:
Yes. A Chrome extension is probably the best choice if your goal is to extract a user's own ChatGPT conversations (with the user's permission) and later expand to Gemini, Claude, etc.

I would not scrape the HTML first. ChatGPT's DOM changes fairly often. Instead, we'll build the extension in layers:

Detect when you're on ChatGPT.

Extract the conversation ID from the URL.

Read the visible messages from the page.

Save them as a normalized JSON object.

Later, we'll replace the message extraction with a more robust network/API interception layer if needed.

